Load test for the RPC runtime
=============================

lt_server and lt_client exercise the runtime and the NDR marshaling code with
many concurrent calls and check every byte of data in both directions.

The interface (loadtest.idl) has one operation per area:

  null, null_idem   empty calls (plain and idempotent)
  struct            scalars of every size, hyper, float/double, enum, fixed
                    array, varying and fixed arrays of strings, string
  array             conformant varying array of structs, in and out
  union             non-encapsulated unions with small/short/long
                    discriminants and an encapsulated union
  list              [unique] linked list in, new list out
  graph             [ptr] (full) pointers with aliasing, in and out
  pipe_in/pipe_out  pipes in both directions
  open/ctx_op/close context handles; the server runs down the contexts of
                    clients that go away
  slow              the manager sleeps (holds a call thread)
  stats             server statistics
  maybe             [maybe] call with an array in (about 14 KB); the next
                    call of the thread follows on the same connection
                    without waiting for it

All data is generated from a key (seed, client id, thread, call number), so
the server checks what the client sent and the client checks the reply.  A
difference is counted as a mismatch and the first ones are printed.

Server
------

  lt_server [-p protseq]... [-e endpoint] [-c max_calls] [-f nofile] [-i secs]

  -p  protocol sequence (default ncacn_ip_tcp; repeat for more)
  -e  fixed endpoint; without it the server uses dynamic endpoints and
      registers them with rpcd, which must be running
  -c  maximum number of concurrent calls (call threads)
  -f  set the open file limit (RLIMIT_NOFILE)
  -i  print statistics every secs seconds

SIGINT or SIGTERM stops the server; it prints its statistics at the end.

Client
------

  lt_client -h host [-e endpoint] [-P tcp|udp|local] [-t threads]
            [-d secs | -n calls] [-r rate] [-m mix] [-z size] [-T ms]
            [-s seed] [-C id] [-W] [-k] [-R] [-S] [-Q] [-v]

Every thread has its own binding handle and therefore its own connection
(association).  -r limits the calls per second of the whole process; -m
selects operations and weights, e.g. -m struct=4,array=2,null=1 (slow and
maybe are only used when -m names them).  -S prints
the server statistics at the end, -Q only prints them.  -k exits without
closing the contexts (the server must run them down).  -P local uses
ncalrpc (needs -e; the host is not used, e.g. -h .).  -W uses union
discriminants that do not fit into 16 bits.  See the comment at the top of
lt_client.c for all options.  The exit status is 0 if all calls succeeded
and all data was correct.

For fault tests (server restarts, network outages), -R makes the client
recover by itself: after a failed call it resets the binding, so that the
endpoint mapper is asked again for a server with a dynamic endpoint,
replaces a context handle whose call failed, and pauses 100 ms.  The line

  outages <n> longest <ms> ms not recovered <k>

of the summary counts the outages of all threads (from the start of the
first failed call to the end of the next successful one), the longest one,
and the threads whose last outage lasted until the end.

"make check" (run_check.sh) runs a short test against a local server on
endpoint 2101 (LT_ENDPOINT) over TCP, UDP and ncalrpc (LT_UDP=0 and
LT_NCALRPC=0 skip them), maybe calls mixed with other calls on the same
connections (the server must run all of them), a context rundown test and
a cancel test; rpcd is not needed.

lt_cancel (Linux only) tests a cancel while a send is blocked: a thread
sends an endless pipe, the server process is stopped (SIGSTOP) so the
socket buffers fill up, and the thread is canceled with a cancel timeout
(-c, default 3 s).  The call must end within that timeout plus 3 s; the
server is continued (SIGCONT) at the end:

  lt_cancel -h 127.0.0.1 -e 2101 -p <pid of lt_server> [-c secs]

Windows client
--------------

lt_client also builds with the Microsoft RPC runtime (MIDL stubs, rpcrt4),
to test Windows clients against a DCE server; lt_port.h/lt_port.c hide the
differences (exceptions, threads, memory of a call, getopt).  With Visual
Studio and the Windows SDK:

  set VCVARS=<path of vcvars64.bat>
  build_win.cmd <output directory>

and run it like the Linux client, e.g.

  lt_client.exe -h <server address> -e 2101 -t 16 -d 60 -S

Without -e the endpoint mapper on the server host (rpcd) resolves the
endpoint.  Windows no longer supports ncadg_ip_udp.

Two hosts
---------

On the server host, start rpcd (port 135, as root) and

  lt_server -i 10

or, without rpcd, lt_server -e 2101.  On the client host(s), start as many
clients as needed, each with a different client id:

  lt_client -h server -t 100 -d 600 -r 50 -C 1 -S

Many client processes with many threads each give many connections.  With
dynamic endpoints the firewall on the server host has to allow port 135 and
the dynamic port range (or use -e and allow only that port).
